WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.755
Light is the fastest thing in the universe.

2
00:00:02.775 --> 00:00:07.524
Yet it took humans about 2,500 years to figure out what it actually is.

3
00:00:07.544 --> 00:00:09.786
Let's speed run the story.

4
00:00:09.806 --> 00:00:14.609
Around 450 BC, Empedocles had a bold idea:

5
00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:18.051
your eyes shoot out invisible fire, like laser beams!

6
00:00:18.071 --> 00:00:21.269
Later, Euclid showed light travels in straight lines

7
00:00:21.289 --> 00:00:24.471
and bounces off mirrors at equal angles.

8
00:00:24.491 --> 00:00:29.704
Around 1021, Ibn al-Haytham, known as Alhazen, flipped it around:

9
00:00:29.724 --> 00:00:31.978
light travels from objects INTO your eye.

10
00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.913
And he tested his ideas with real experiments.

11
00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:37.606
Many call him the father of optics.

12
00:00:37.626 --> 00:00:46.052
1665: Grimaldi notices shadows have fuzzy, striped edges. He names it diffraction.

13
00:00:46.072 --> 00:00:50.129
Isaac Newton splits white light into a rainbow with a prism,

14
00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:53.242
and declares: light is made of tiny particles!

15
00:00:53.262 --> 00:01:01.416
He once poked a needle behind his own eyeball to see colors. Do not try this in lab.

16
00:01:01.436 --> 00:01:04.808
Christiaan Huygens disagrees: light is a WAVE!

17
00:01:04.828 --> 00:01:07.876
Every point on a wave launches new little wavelets.

18
00:01:07.896 --> 00:01:11.682
Remember this idea. It is the heart of diffraction.

19
00:01:11.702 --> 00:01:15.296
But Newton was Newton, so particles won for 100 years.

20
00:01:15.316 --> 00:01:19.571
Then in 1801, Thomas Young sends light through two tiny slits.

21
00:01:19.591 --> 00:01:22.828
Result: stripes! Bright, dark, bright, dark.

22
00:01:22.848 --> 00:01:26.943
Particles can't do that. Waves can. Waves interfere.

23
00:01:26.963 --> 00:01:31.446
1818: Augustin Fresnel builds the full math of diffraction.

24
00:01:31.466 --> 00:01:38.119
His critic Poisson mocks it: “That predicts a BRIGHT spot in the middle of a round shadow!”

25
00:01:38.139 --> 00:01:40.970
Arago does the experiment... and there it is.

26
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.336
The bright spot. Oops.

27
00:01:43.356 --> 00:01:49.540
1865: James Clerk Maxwell writes the equations of electricity and magnetism.

28
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.073
He calculates the speed of their waves... and gets the speed of light!

29
00:01:53.093 --> 00:01:59.969
Radio, infrared, visible, UV, X-rays: all the same thing, different wavelengths.

30
00:01:59.989 --> 00:02:01.754
Case closed? Not quite.

31
00:02:01.774 --> 00:02:08.289
1905: Albert Einstein shows light hits metal in little packets of energy: photons.

32
00:02:08.309 --> 00:02:12.060
Particles are back! The real answer: light is BOTH.

33
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.309
Now the part for materials people.

34
00:02:14.329 --> 00:02:20.162
1912: Max von Laue shoots X-rays at a crystal and gets a pattern of spots.

35
00:02:20.182 --> 00:02:23.168
The rows of atoms act like a diffraction grating!

36
00:02:23.188 --> 00:02:32.851
1913: father and son Bragg write  nλ = 2d sinθ  to measure atomic spacing.

37
00:02:32.871 --> 00:02:36.519
Lawrence won the Nobel Prize at age 25.

38
00:02:36.539 --> 00:02:42.067
1924: de Broglie says matter is wavy too. Soon, electrons diffract!

39
00:02:42.087 --> 00:02:46.794
J.J. Thomson won a Nobel for showing the electron is a particle.

40
00:02:46.814 --> 00:02:49.320
His son G.P. won one for showing it is a wave.

41
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.284
Family dinners must have been interesting.

42
00:02:52.304 --> 00:02:59.081
Today: lasers, optical fibers, and giant X-ray sources that watch atoms move.

43
00:02:59.101 --> 00:03:00.752
So here is your question:

44
00:03:00.772 --> 00:03:04.403
How can a wave reveal the spacing of atoms you cannot see?

45
00:03:04.423 --> 00:03:07.389
Let's talk about DIFFRACTION.
